      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "Given that $n$ leaves a remainder of $1$ when divided by $6$, we can express this relationship as:\n\\[ n = 6k + 1 \\]\nwhere $k$ is an integer.\n\nWe are asked to find the remainder when $n + 2010$ is divided by $6$. Substituting the expression for $n$ into this, we get:\n\\[ n + 2010 = (6k + 1) + 2010 = 6k + 2011 \\]\n\nNow, we need to find the remainder when $6k + 2011$ is divided by $6$. Since $6k$ is always divisible by $6$, it does not affect the remainder of $2011$ when divided by $6$. Thus, we only need to find the remainder of $2011$ when divided by $6$.\n\nLet's compute $2011 \\mod 6$:\n\\[ 2011 \\div 6 = 335 \\text{ with a remainder } 1 \\]\nSo,\n\\[ 2011 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{6} \\]\n\nTherefore,\n\\[ 6k + 2011 \\equiv 6k + 1 \\pmod{6} \\]\n\nSince $6k$ is divisible by $6$, it does not affect the remainder. Hence,\n\\[ 6k + 2011 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{6} \\]\n\nTherefore, the remainder when $n + 2010$ is divided by $6$ is $\\boxed{1}$.",
